## Creating a new Site

A site is a reusable place record. Collecting events belong to a site, and
specimen capture records reach the site through their collecting event. Create
one site for each distinct camp, trap line, locality, or other place used by
your project.

{% steps %}
1. Navigate to the site page using the bottom navigation bar.

2. Use the `+` button at the top right corner to create a new site.

    You can also use the three-dot menu icon and tap `Create site`.

    {% aside type="tip" %}

    You can minimize repeated entry by duplicating a site instead of creating a
    new one. Open the three-dot menu and choose `Duplicate site`. NAHPU copies
    the site information, but leaves the `Site ID` and coordinates empty. Enter
    a unique ID and add the correct coordinates before saving.

    {% /aside %}

3. Create a site ID.



    We recommend creating a site ID convention for your team. An example of site ID convention for mammalogy fieldwork:

    - City, town, or village is ID-ed as it is. For example, the city of Bogor will be `BOGOR`.
    - The camp site uses `{2-letter locality abbreviation}-CAMP{camp number}` format. For example, camp 1 in Mt. Gede-Pangrango is `GP-CAMP1`.
    - The trap line uses `{2-letter locality abbreviation}{trapline leader initial}-L{line number}` format. For example, line 1 in Mt. Gede-Pangrango led by Heru Handika is `GPHH-L1`.
    - The pitfall line uses `{2-letter locality abbreviation}{pitfall line leader initial}-P{line number}` format. For example, line 1 in Mt. Gede-Pangrango led by Heru Handika is `GPHH-P1`.

    {% aside type="tip" %}

    For a project involving multiple devices, use the same `Site ID` for an
    identical site. This makes exchanged site and event records easier to match
    when they are imported into another project device.

    {% /aside %}

4. Add the Site leader

    If this is a field camp, you can put the team leader name. If it is a trap line, you can design a person in-charge of that trap line as the site leader.

5. Add the site type

    The site type categorizes the site by its characteristics or use. Choose a
    value from the project's configured site-type list.

6. Fill in the geography details

    We recommend to follow your museum convention for writing the precise locality and remarks.

    {% aside type="tip" title="Writing remarks" %}

    If the site is a trapline, it is a good practice to explain how the trapline is run.

    For example:

    ```text
    The trapline runs from Shelter I to Shelter IV along the trails betweeen the to camp sites.
    ```

    {% /aside %}

7. Fill in the habitat information

    - Type: The type of habitats: plantation, lowland rainforest, upper montane forest, etc.
    - Condition: The condition of the habitat: disturbed, undisturbed, minimal disturbance, etc.
    - Description: A brief description of the habitat. For example:

      ```text
      Dominated by oak trees. The ground was mostly covered by ferns, gingers, and oak seedlings.

      ```

{% /steps %}

## Site information

The site form records the place and the people responsible for it:

- `Site ID`: a project-unique identifier used by events and exchanged records.
- `Site leader`: the person responsible for the camp, trap line, or other site.
- `Site type`: the configured category for the site.
- Locality and remarks: precise locality text and notes about how the site is
  used or operated.
- Habitat type, condition, and description: the environment observed at the
  site.

## Managing sites

Open the three-dot menu on the site page for these actions:

- `Create site`: create a new site record.
- `Duplicate site`: copy the site details into a new record with a blank ID and
  blank coordinates.
- `Copy from project ...`: copy selected site information from a site in another
  project into the current empty site.
- `Show QR`: display the site record for another mobile device to scan.
- `Export site`: save the site record as JSON or compresed in a ZIP/TAR.GZ file for records with media attachments.
    The exported file can be imported into another device or shared with other team members.
    {% aside type="tip" %}
    For large site records or for importing site into another device without a camera access, use this option. You can then transfer the file to another device and import it there.
    {% /aside %}
- `Scan QR`: create or replace a site from a site QR code.
- `Import site`: create or replace a site from an exported JSON record.
- `Delete record`: delete the current site. A site cannot be deleted while an
  event still references it.
- `Delete all records`: delete all sites in the current project. Events that
  reference those sites must be removed first.

When importing a site, choose whether the imported record creates a new site or
updates an existing site. Review linked personnel and coordinates after an
import.

## Copy site data from another project

Use `Copy from project ...` when a site in another project has information that
you want to reuse in the current project. This action fills the current site;
it does not create a new site or change the source project.

The current site must be empty before you begin. A site with information,
coordinates, media, or linked data cannot receive copied data. At least two
projects must exist, because the source must be a different project.

{% steps %}

1. Open the site that will receive the copied information. Make sure it is an
   empty site record.

2. Open the three-dot menu and select `Copy from project ...`.

3. Select the source project by name, then select one of its sites.

4. Select the data to copy. The available choices include:

   - Site ID
   - Site leader
   - Site type
   - Geography fields, including country, state/province, county, municipality,
     and precise locality
   - Remarks
   - Habitat type, condition, and description
   - Coordinates

   Fields without data in the source site are unavailable. Selecting
   `Coordinates` copies all coordinates belonging to the selected source site.

5. Select `Copy` to fill the current site.

{% /steps %}

The source site remains unchanged. This action copies site fields and
coordinates only; it does not copy site media, associated-data links,
fossil-site records, collecting events, or other records linked to the source
site.

{% aside type="tip" title="If copying is unavailable" %}

If NAHPU reports that multiple projects are required, create or import another
project first. If it reports that the site is not empty, create a new site or
remove the existing site information, coordinates, media, and linked data
before trying again.

{% /aside %}

## Adding coordinates

Each site can have multiple coordinate records for trapline ends, separate
collection points, or other points that belong to the same site. Add a
coordinate manually, use the device GPS, or import points from a GIS/GPS file.

### Use device GPS while adding a coordinate

{% steps %}

1. On the site page, open the coordinate panel and tap/click `Add coordinate`.

2. On the `Add coordinates` page, use the `Device GPS` card and tap/click its
   location button.

3. NAHPU fills the decimal latitude, decimal longitude, elevation, and GPS
   uncertainty when the operating system provides them. Review the values,
   complete the remaining fields, and save the coordinate.

    Enter the GPS unit name separately, using the exact model of your device,
    such as `iPad Mini 4`.

{% /steps %}

### Manually adding coordinate

{% steps %}

1. On the site page at the coordinate panel, tap/click on the `Add coordinate` button.

2. Enter the coordinate name.

    Keep the name short and informative. You can use the `Notes` field to add additional information about the coordinate.

    {% aside type="tip" %}

    We recommend creating a coordinate name convention for your team. Example of coordinate convention for mammalogy:

      - `{siteID}S` for a coordinate taken at the start of the trapline, such as `KMHH-L1S`.
      - `{siteID}C` for the coordinate taken at the center of the trapline.
      - `{siteID}E` for a coordinate taken at the end of the trapline, such as `KMHH-L1E`.
      - If the trapline extends beyond the first start point, the coordinate at the new start is suffixed with `{number}`. For example, `KMHH-L1S2` for first extension, `KMHH-L1S3` for the next extension, and so on until the trapline are fully moved.
      - If the trapline extends beyond the first end point, the coordinate at the new end is suffixed with `{number}`. For example, `KMHH-L1E2` for first extension, and continue in a similar manner as the start line extension.

    {% /aside %}

3. Select a coordinate format and enter its values.

    NAHPU accepts decimal degrees (DD), degrees and decimal minutes (DDM),
    degrees-minutes-seconds (DMS), and a single UTM value in the form
    `zone hemisphere easting northing`. UTM input uses WGS84. Latitude and
    longitude directions must match their axes, and converted decimal values
    must be within valid latitude and longitude ranges.

    For DDM and DMS, enter degrees, minutes, and seconds in their separate
    numeric fields, then choose N/S for latitude and E/W for longitude. NAHPU
    adds the coordinate symbols and concatenates the parts for the Darwin Core
    verbatim latitude and longitude values.

    NAHPU saves decimal latitude and longitude for calculations and also keeps
    the concatenated value and coordinate system for Darwin Core verbatim
    fields. If coordinate values are already entered, changing formats asks
    for confirmation before clearing them. Selecting DD clears older verbatim
    coordinate values after the coordinate is saved.

4. Add elevation in meters.

5. Select the datum.

    The default datum is WGS84. Check your GPS settings for the exact datum of your coordinates.

6. Enter uncertainty values in meters.

    Watch the uncertainty of your GPS. Some old GPS may not record the number. It is a good practice to take note of the uncertainty if you do not bring the NAHPU device with you to the coordinate location.

7. Enter the name of the GPS you use.

    Example device name: `Garmin 62sc`

8. Add notes.

    Add additional information if any.

{% /steps %}

### Import coordinates from a file

Use file import when several points already exist in a GIS or GPS file.
Coordinate import is available from `Add coordinate → Import` on a site.

{% steps %}

1. Open a site, tap/click `Add coordinate`, and select the `Import` mode.

2. The supported formats appear above the import actions. Choose
   `Select coordinate file` and select a GeoJSON/JSON file, KML file, zipped
   Shapefile, or GPX file. You can also choose `Scan QR` to add a coordinate QR
   record from another NAHPU device.

3. Review the imported points and any warning text. The map highlights the
   focused point, and all valid points are selected by default.

4. Use `Select all`, `Clear all`, or the individual checkboxes to choose which
   points to add. Tap/click `Add N selected` to save them to the current site.

{% /steps %}

File import transfers the point name, notes, decimal latitude, decimal
longitude, and elevation. Imported points are assigned the current site and
`WGS84` datum; uncertainty and GPS unit information are not supplied by this
exchange flow.

Only point coordinates are imported. Non-point GeoJSON or Shapefile features,
non-point KML geometries, invalid coordinates, and other unsupported records
are skipped and reported in the review. GPX waypoints are imported; GPX routes
and tracks are ignored. If no valid points remain, NAHPU reports an import
error instead of adding records.

### Manage and export coordinates

The `Manage` button in a site's coordinate panel opens all project coordinates.
You can filter the list by site, select individual points or all visible
points, focus a point on the map, and open the map in full screen.

{% steps %}

1. Open a site's coordinate panel and tap/click `Manage`.

2. Filter by site if needed, then select the coordinates to export.

3. Tap/click `Export coordinates (N)`.

4. Choose `GeoJSON (.geojson)`, `KML (.kml)`, or `Shapefile bundle (.zip)`.
   Enter a file name, choose a destination directory, and select `Export`.

5. After the file is created, use `Share` on devices that support sharing.

{% /steps %}

Every exported coordinate must have a valid decimal latitude and longitude.
GIS exports include the coordinate name, notes, WGS84 decimal latitude and
longitude, and elevation when available. Coordinate export is separate from
`Export records`, project bundles, and database backups; use those options when
you need tabular records or a restorable NAHPU project copy.
